When demos show different output and input dimensions, establish the output geometry separately from the cell-content rule. Never presume that output preserves input dimensions or infer the shape from a salient portion of the test. Record each pair's exact input/output dimensions and test the proposed structural mapping against all examples.

Before submission, mechanically check row count, each row's width, and legal cell values. If evaluator feedback says the grid was not a valid ARC grid, treat that first as a dimension/serialization failure—not semantic evidence—and repair the structural output shape before another submission. In strict action tasks, make this audit before emitting the JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/output-shape-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/repeated-motif-extension.md", "content": "# Repeated motif, continuation, and extension: review notes

A single demonstration showed a structured foreground motif repeated in a second location; only a region at the later instance's edge changed to color 1. Other demonstrations showed the same broad palette but dissimilar geometry and localized changed regions. This does not establish a reliable shared operation by itself: the hypothesis that color 1 simply fills a horizontal run from the rightmost marks failed on the test, as did a second attempt that filled apparent gaps between sparse cells.

For future analogous tasks, compare exact repeated foreground masks before interpreting each changed run independently. Test whether copies differ by a precise template completion, periodic continuation, or directional extension, and verify the candidates against all demos and negative evidence. Do not use these session examples as a solved rule. After an incorrect grid, discard the complete inferred transformation and reconstruct deltas anew. Spend demos on an explicit discriminator and retain the exact one-action JSON contract.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/repeated-motif-extension.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-lessons", "file": "references/noisy-motif-projection.md", "content": "# Sparse motif projection: review lesson

A demo had a diagonal multi-color marker pattern, while the test contained a compact related motif. The response guessed a single local color adjustment without establishing the transformation. Generalizable lesson: derive the full rule from all changed and unchanged demo coordinates, then apply that operation to the entire test motif; do not preserve most input cells merely because they appear unrelated. Where the demo adds diagonal runs, enumerate the rays and their bounds explicitly and distinguish them from row/column continuation or marker replacement. This is a caution about the analysis method, not a confirmed rule for the particular puzzle.
